     "en": "A short story"
    },
    "text": {
     "en": "Ravi hammered a piece of copper and a piece of coal.\n\"The copper flattens, the coal breaks,\" he said."
    }
   },
   {
    "id": "explain",
    "type": "explanation",
    "provenance": "original",
    "keepEnglishTerms": [
     "malleable",
     "ductile",
     "sonorous"
    ],
    "text": {
     "en": "Metals are malleable, which means they can be beaten into thin sheets, like aluminium foil. They are ductile, so they can be drawn into wires, like copper wire.\nMetals are sonorous, producing a ringing sound when struck, like a bell. They conduct heat and electricity well, so we make pans and wires of metals.\nNon-metals like coal and sulphur are brittle and poor conductors, except graphite."
    }
